00:00:02.360 --> 00:00:04.370 align:start position:0%
the main asteroid belt between Mars and  Jupiter is home to millions of  meteoroids but how did these objects  form in the first place  astronomers have two ideas one thought  is that asteroid ancestors also known as  planetesimals became large through  gradual accretion grabbing more and more  material over time like a snowball  rolling down a hill the other idea is  that asteroids were made by a  gravitational collapse that the space  dusts that makes up the asteroid smash  together almost instantaneously because  eons of collisions have broken apart  these original asteroids scientists  aren't sure which one of these series is  true but they've made a big leap toward  proving the latter similar asteroids  have sorted into groups called families  researchers selected a family of  asteroids known for their low  reflectivity in an area of the belt  where there's a large amount of brighter  asteroids each of these dark asteroids  were in a slightly different orbit but  when mapped out the asteroids were in a  v-shaped formation  unlike the brighter asteroids nearby  this pattern was more spread out not  only does this mean that more collisions  happen but that radiation pressure from  sunlight shifted their orbits gradually  over time the extent of the spread means  that these dark asteroids have been  around longer than middle lighter  neighbors the researcher suggests the  original dark planetesimals were  probably formed four billion years ago  making this one of the oldest asteroid  families in the main belt because the  researchers determined that the original  planetesimals were no smaller than 35  kilometers across  they say this is compelling evidence for  gravitational collapse because it's  close to the size average needed for the  hypothesis to work but for now there's  still a lot of work to be done before we  know the true origin of our companions  in the belt  [Music]  [Music]
 

